WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:03.790
Now that we have finished our grass,

2
00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:05.870
let's talk about physical materials.

3
00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:09.189
Now, physical materials allow you to assign

4
00:00:09.189 --> 00:00:10.769
different physical properties

5
00:00:10.769 --> 00:00:13.630
to various surfaces in your project.

6
00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:17.550
And a surface could be anything like a static mesh,

7
00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.190
or in our case, we're gonna show you

8
00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:20.710
how to assign surfaces

9
00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:23.510
inside of your material via textures.

10
00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:25.850
Now, the cool thing about physical materials

11
00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:30.030
is they can not only represent things like density

12
00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.090
or how bouncy something is,

13
00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:37.090
but they can also be used to do things like spawn particles

14
00:00:37.289 --> 00:00:40.609
or spawn different sounds when a user encounters

15
00:00:40.609 --> 00:00:42.409
or interacts with different surfaces.

16
00:00:42.409 --> 00:00:43.929
For us, what we're gonna do is

17
00:00:43.929 --> 00:00:46.729
we are just going to make two physical materials.

18
00:00:46.729 --> 00:00:48.810
We're gonna make one for our dirt

19
00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:50.289
and one for our grass.

20
00:00:50.289 --> 00:00:52.049
So let's go ahead and pop over to the editor

21
00:00:52.049 --> 00:00:52.890
and do that now.

22
00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.289
So I'm gonna hit Control space,

23
00:00:54.289 --> 00:00:55.850
and I'm just gonna right-click inside of here.

24
00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:57.409
I'm gonna go to physics,

25
00:00:57.429 --> 00:01:00.229
and then we're gonna come down here to physical material.

26
00:01:00.229 --> 00:01:01.270
Now, when I do this,

27
00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:03.829
I am gonna be asked what physical material class

28
00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:04.909
I wanna base this off of.

29
00:01:04.909 --> 00:01:08.909
So we're gonna select physical material and click select.

30
00:01:08.909 --> 00:01:13.150
We're gonna give this a new name of pm underscore dirt.

31
00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:14.390
And then instead of doing that again,

32
00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:15.670
I'm just gonna right-click on this

33
00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:20.739
and duplicate and call this new one grass.

34
00:01:20.739 --> 00:01:24.939
And then I'm just going to save both of those.

35
00:01:24.939 --> 00:01:27.340
So the next thing that we need to do with this

36
00:01:27.359 --> 00:01:28.939
is inside of our material,

37
00:01:28.939 --> 00:01:32.879
there is a landscape physical material output node.

38
00:01:32.879 --> 00:01:34.159
And with this node,

39
00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:36.640
what we can do is bring it into our graph.

40
00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:38.239
And then in the details panel,

41
00:01:38.239 --> 00:01:40.659
we can add as many inputs

42
00:01:40.659 --> 00:01:44.000
as we have physical materials that need this.

43
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.739
Now, one thing to note here,

44
00:01:45.739 --> 00:01:47.120
we wanna try to keep these things

45
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.599
as optimized as possible.

46
00:01:48.599 --> 00:01:52.519
So if you have five or six materials in your landscape,

47
00:01:52.519 --> 00:01:55.120
but you really only need two or three of those

48
00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.719
to actually have physical materials,

49
00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:58.260
that's the best way to approach this.

50
00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:00.299
Remember, less is always better

51
00:02:00.299 --> 00:02:02.180
when you're dealing with real-time applications.

52
00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:04.840
So let's go ahead and pop open our material

53
00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.299
and add this node and get it ready to be set up.

54
00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:10.949
So we're gonna come over here to our matte landscape.

55
00:02:10.949 --> 00:02:11.830
There we go.

56
00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:14.030
And then I'm just gonna come up here

57
00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:17.590
to where my BC dirt and my BC grass are,

58
00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:18.449
and I'm gonna right-click

59
00:02:18.449 --> 00:02:20.849
and we're gonna look for our landscape.

60
00:02:20.849 --> 00:02:22.590
And we're gonna come down here

61
00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:26.550
and look for our landscape physical material outputs.

62
00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:28.569
We're gonna go ahead and grab that.

63
00:02:28.569 --> 00:02:30.750
And then, like I said before, in the details panel,

64
00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:34.689
we're gonna add one and two elements to this.

65
00:02:34.689 --> 00:02:37.250
And if we just come here and scroll down,

66
00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:42.250
we'll see our PM dirt and our PM grass.

67
00:02:44.819 --> 00:02:46.400
Now we are gonna get this message

68
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.900
that the physical material input is missing

69
00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:51.099
and we have it twice because we have two inputs here.

70
00:02:51.099 --> 00:02:53.939
So what we're actually gonna do with this

71
00:02:53.939 --> 00:02:58.060
is we're now going to go to our BC dirt

72
00:02:58.879 --> 00:02:59.879
and our BC grass textures

73
00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:01.280
and actually plug those

74
00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.759
right into this physical material output node.

75
00:03:04.759 --> 00:03:07.280
Yes, it is literally that simple.

76
00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:08.919
All we have to do to define

77
00:03:08.919 --> 00:03:12.199
which part of our landscape material we want to have,

78
00:03:12.199 --> 00:03:14.080
this physics or this physics,

79
00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.599
is literally just wire that texture

80
00:03:16.599 --> 00:03:19.560
into this respective node and we will get that.

81
00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:20.900
So let's go ahead and do that now.

82
00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.080
We're gonna pop back over here to the editor

83
00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:26.219
and we're just gonna take our BC dirt, whoops,

84
00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:29.039
and we're gonna take the output of our BC dirt, there we go,

85
00:03:29.039 --> 00:03:30.840
and then we're gonna do the same thing for our grass.

86
00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.000
We're just gonna plug that in

87
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.300
and we're going to hit apply and save.

88
00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:38.680
All right, now, in order to check this,

89
00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:42.240
what we can do is inside of our console,

90
00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:43.960
we can input the following command,

91
00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:48.960
landscape.collisionmesh.showphysicalmaterial equal,

92
00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:53.039
or you can say equal to one or one.

93
00:03:53.039 --> 00:03:54.860
And what this is going to do

94
00:03:54.879 --> 00:03:57.360
is this is actually going to allow us

95
00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:01.840
to see the differences where the physical material

96
00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.240
for one texture is used

97
00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:06.879
versus the physical material for another texture,

98
00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:08.120
and we can see that right here.

99
00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.080
So let's go ahead and just pop back in it

100
00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:11.879
to our editor right here.

101
00:04:11.879 --> 00:04:13.560
And in order to do this, first off,

102
00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.860
we're gonna hit Alt C on the keyboard,

103
00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:18.439
and then I'm gonna paste this command right in here

104
00:04:18.439 --> 00:04:20.360
and press enter, and there we go.

105
00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:22.680
That is showing you the difference

106
00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:24.279
between those two physical materials.

107
00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:26.980
So we can see here, one is right here

108
00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:28.500
and then the other is right there.

109
00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:31.420
And then kind of as they are mixing through this,

110
00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:34.139
we are getting different values there.

111
00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:37.759
So this wraps it up for this portion

112
00:04:37.759 --> 00:04:39.100
of our video tutorial.
